**The Universe**

Let us believe one of the modern hypotheses, in which the following periodization of the development of the Universe is proposed:

a) inflationary stage ($t_1 = 10^{-34}\ \text{s} \div t_2 = 10^{-32}\ \text{s}$);

b) radiation domination ($t_2 = 10^{-32}\ \text{s} \div t_3 = 70000$ years);

c) dust stage ($t_3 = 70000$ years $\div\ t_4 = 7.9$ billion years);

d) $\Lambda$-domination ($t_4 = 7.9$ billion years $\div\ t_5 =$ present time).

By what factor is the scale factor $a(t)$ at the end of each of the four periods listed above larger than at its beginning?

Use in the solution the values of the following quantities:

density during inflation $\rho_\mathrm{inf} = 6.6\cdot 10^{76}\ \dfrac{\text{kg}}{\text{m}^3}$;

density of the $\Lambda$-term: $\rho_\Lambda = 8.51\cdot 10^{-27}\ \dfrac{\text{kg}}{\text{m}^3}$.

Hint: use the first line of the Internet.

