**Determination of the distance to a galaxy cluster**

The table gives the values of the distance to the cluster of galaxies in the constellation Virgo, determined by different methods.

$$\begin{array}{|c|l|c|}\hline \text{No.} & \text{Distance determined from:} & \text{Distance (Mpc)} \\ \hline 1 & \text{Globular clusters} & 18.8\pm3.8 \\ \hline 2 & \text{Faber–Jackson relation} & 16.8\pm2.4 \\ \hline 3 & \text{Planetary nebulae} & 15.2\pm1.1 \\ \hline 4 & \text{Novae} & 21.1\pm3.9 \\ \hline 5 & \text{Surface brightness fluctuations} & 15.9\pm0.9 \\ \hline 6 & \text{Type Ia supernovae} & 19.4\pm5.0 \\ \hline 7 & \text{Tully–Fisher relation} & 15.8\pm1.5 \\ \hline 8 & \text{Cepheids} & 14.9\pm1.2 \\ \hline \end{array}$$

Using the given data, determine, taking the weighting coefficient into account:

a) the mean value of the distance to this cluster;

b) the error (standard deviation) of all the measurements;

c) the distance to the cluster of galaxies;

d) using the results obtained, determine the value of the Hubble constant, if the redshift in the spectra of the galaxies of the cluster is $z = 0.38\%$.
